  journal                 = {Applied Acoustics},
  title = {An adaptive synchroextracting transform for the analysis of noise contaminated multi-component nonstationary signals},
  year                    = {2023},
  issn                    = {0003682X},
  note                    = {cited By 4},
  volume                  = {202},
  abbrev_source_title = {Appl Acoust},
  abstract                = {The Synchro-Extracting Transform technique (SET) can capture the changing dynamic in a non-stationary signal which can be applied for fault diagnosis of rotating machinery operating under varying speed or/and load conditions. However, the time frequency representation (TFR) of a signal produced by SET can be affected by noise contained in the signal, which can largely reduce the accuracy of fault diagnosis. This paper addresses this drawback and presents a new extraction operator to improve the energy concentration of the TFR of a noise contaminated multi-component signal by using an adaptive ridge curve identification process together with SET. The adaptive ridge curve extraction is deployed to extract the signal components of a multi-component signal via an iterative approach. The effectiveness of the algorithm is verified using one set of simulated noise-added signals and two sets of experimental bearing and gearbox defect signals. The result shows that the proposed technique can accurately identify the fault components from noise contaminated multi-component non-stationary machine defect signals. © 2022 Elsevier Ltd},
